This was based on the 3.15 PR: https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/pull/43129/files

Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

This is a refinement of the [problem I reported in `union`](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/85054#issuecomment-5435102806), but it applies to pre-existing syntax rather than being tied to `union`, a new feature; definitely `switch` - possibly also `if`/`else` expressions?

**Version Used**: 

all current, but: `#error` reports

> Error CS8304 : Compiler version: '5.10.0-1.26381.103 (e2c1e00b3d0f96afb892fb261d5921565b400246)'. Language version: preview. Compiler path: 'C:\Program Files\dotnet\sdk\11.0.100-preview.7.26381.103\Roslyn\bincore\Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.dll'.

Also tested with a few other lang-vers, no change.

**Steps to Reproduce**:

The following outputs

```
array element type: System.UInt32
int[4]
int[4]
```

This means that both approaches have hit the `int[]` arm, and in both cases the `uint[]` arm is silently unreachable, as demonstrated by the fact that the thing we're passing in *is exactly a `uint[]`*

``` c#
uint[] arr = {0,2,3,4}; // definitely is a uint[]
Console.WriteLine($"array element type: {arr.GetType().GetElementType()}");
Console.WriteLine(WhatAmIStatement(arr));
Console.WriteLine(WhatAmIExpression(arr));

static string WhatAmIStatement(object? array)
{
    switch (array)
    {
        case int[] i: return $"int[{i.Length}]";
        case uint[] u: return $"uint[{u.Length}]";
        case IConvertible c: return c.GetType().ToString(); // to force int to be unreachable
        // case int x: return "int"; // unreachable, CS8120 (error)
        case {} obj: return obj.GetType().ToString(); // to make default unreachable
        case null: return "(null)";
        default: return "???"; // unreachable, CS0162 (warning)
    }
}
static string WhatAmIExpression(object? array)
{
    return array switch
    {
        int[] i => $"int[{i.Length}]",
        uint[] u => $"uint[{u.Length}]",
        IConvertible c => c.GetType().ToString(), // to force int to be unreachable
        // int x => "int", // unreachable, CS8510 (error)
        { } obj => obj.GetType().ToString(), // to make default unreachable
        null => "(null)",
        // _ => "???", // unreachable, CS8510 (error)
    };
}
```

**Diagnostic Id**:

I would *expect* some choice from CS8120, CS8510, CS0162 - probably CS8510 in the expression case, and CS8120 in the statement case, for parity with their peer examples.

**Expected Behavior**:

An unreachable diagnostic is emitted. This expectation is reinforced by the other unreachable examples shown in the sample - `int` after `IConvertible`, and a default after both `null` and `{}`.

**Actual Behavior**:

No diagnostic; the `uint[]` arm is silently unreachable.
